ady signed off on the  deal in announcing his approval in  february the transport minister said  the devastating impact of covet 19 on  the airline industry was a key  factor this would have created a much  larger  airline this airline industry analyst  says getting approval from the ec  likely would have meant giving up routes  to europe so as not to be too strong  competing with other carriers  that would have been one hurdle too many  for air canada  if they had to shrink in order to  complete this merger it probably didn't  make sense for our canada to do it  now to pull out of the deal air canada's  had to make some concessions  it has to pay air trans at a 12.5  million  termination payment it's also waiving a  10 million dollar fee  it would have been paid if air transact  were to find a new buyer  there may be one out there quebec media  mogul pierre carl pelado made his own  offer for the company in december and is  confirming it's still on the table  air transit said friday it's considering  pursuing those discussions  now the federal government is already  saying it's in talks with air transit  and  focused on protecting jobs there will  also be pressure on the quebec  government to support the company its  headquarters are in montreal but that  may be  slightly more delicate quebec's premier  has some ties to the company  about 30 years ago francois lego is one  of its  founders mike armstrong global news  montreal  the head of the minneapolis homicide  unit on the witness stand coming up  what he testified about derrick shoven  keeping his knee on george floyd's neck  there was some tense testimony at the  murder trial of former minneapolis  police officer derrick  chauvin today a high-ranking officer  called chauvin's use of force on george  floyd last may  totally unnecessary pulling him down to  the ground face down  and putting your knee on the neck for  that amount of that amount of time  it's just um uncalled for  um i saw no reason why  the officers felt they were in danger if  that's what they felt  and that's what they would have to feel  to be able to use that kind of force  so in your opinion should that restraint  have stopped once he was handcuffed  and prone on the ground absolutely  part of chauvin's defense is that he was  just doing what he was trained to do  the high profile murder trial resumes on  monday  there are signs tonight of a possible  return to talks on the iran nuclear deal  washington and tehran have agreed to  start  indirect talks with other major world  powers next week  this comes nearly three years after  president donald trump pulled the u.s  out of the deal president biden has said  rejoining the agreement is a priority  for his  administration negotiations are expected  to be difficult there are a number of  issues to be discussed including  sanctions  so far the u.s and iran are not expected  to have any direct  talks vaccinations and violence still  ahead the risks  facing frontline workers in war zones  terrible scenes in taiwan after an  express train with 500 people on board  derailed in a tunnel  some cars were ripped apart others  crumpled at least 51 people are dead  including the driver and more than 140  are injured  the train hit a truck that had rolled  onto the tracks from a work site above  some survivors escaped the wreck through  the windows or through the roof  the crash happened at the start of a  traditional long weekend holiday when  people head home to tend  to family graves aid workers in conflict  zones always do  dangerous work they provide critical  care while facing grave risks  just this week three women vaccinating  children against polio were shot and  killed in afghanistan  as countries around the world prepare to  roll out max kovid 19 vaccinations  crystal commansing looks at the risks to  the people doing it  the site of a coffin in a conflict zone  is not unusual but it doesn't make it  any easier to see  two separate shootings on tuesday led to  the deaths of three polio vaccination  workers  international laws protect health care  workers in conflict zones  still jason nickerson says the job can  be dangerous  if you're providing health care uh close  to front lines  that is inherently risky in a way that  working in a hospital in downtown ottawa  is is just not nickerson who is in  ottawa right now  recently spent time in the democratic  republic of congo  working to prevent the spread of ebola  covid19 vaccinations are a part of the  work being done by doctors without  borders  in lebanon local ceasefires are going to  be essential to enable  life-saving vaccinations to take place  britain's foreign secretary  successfully lobbied the u.n security  council to call on all nations  engaged in conflicts to pause for  humanitarian efforts  it's not that fear of covet 19  vaccinations would  increase the risk to workers but rather  an acknowledgment  of their critical roles  360 thousand doses of covid19 vaccine  recently arrived in yemen a country  plagued by a multi-sided civil war  that has raged for years if you see all  the misery on the ground if you see  people underneath on the ground  you can understand why we're doing this  work bhagwasetawan says risk management  is a critical part of his work with  oxfam in yemen  but he says people are the most  important part to him  you see how people are smiling you see  how people  appreciating the support given workers  regardless of their task or the logo on  their backs  say the need is too great to focus on  the risk  krystal gamansen global news london  autism across cultures next how race is  impacting the care of children  on the spectrum  you're watching global national  [Music]  the driver of this school bus didn't  even have time to react a deer  smashed through the front windshield  landing on a student who was hunched  over asleep  in the front seat it happened in  virginia the 15 year old in that seat  said afterwards he was stunned and  really confused but  he is okay and so is the deer which ran  off  when the bus driver opened the door  today is world autism awareness day  raising a child with autism can be  challenging and sometimes difficult  and as mike draulie explains adding  cultural differences  into the mix can make it even harder for  parents to find the support  their kids need for xavier jackson the  first hints of autism became apparent  at camp they said they can't handle him  i should come pick him up but it was  when he started kindergarten that his  mother  jane knew they needed help in his second  week he was suspended  because he had one of his educators  xavier was four when he was diagnosed  with autism and when she sought help she  says they found a system that  ignored multiculturalism eventually she  found a single session devoted to the  black community  there was also a punjabi session that i  attended because  they were talking about feelings of  shame  and i you know i said i can relate to  that  dr shivajan savapalan says we can't  ignore those cultural issues anymore  indeed language and culture were factors  in a 2017 study  that found black children are 19 percent  less likely than white children  to be diagnosed with autism before dr  sevapalan's autism clinic began  operating a mobile service to  marginalized communities  he would direct families to their  pediatricians  but then they would show up a year later  still with the same issues and i'm  realizing there is a lot of  you know falling through the gaps  foundations like camille proctor's the  color of autism aims to fix that  by doing outreach programs into  communities that are distrustful of  authority  and new to the idea autism doesn't see  race  initially the response was like why are  you trying to do something separate for  separate people this is segregation  and i'm like it's really not  proctor who is from detroit knows it's  an uncomfortable topic  especially in the current political  climate funding is also an issue as it's  always been with autism  but even if services aren't expanded in  the us and canada  education programs can be so there's so  many things that i think we all could do  to make this world better  i'm just trying to do my little work  mike lake global news toronto  and that is global national for this  friday i'm donna friesen if you've got  children who can't wait for an easter  egg hunt we want to show you some  animals getting  in early on the fun meerkats and monkeys  at the london zoo had their own easter  egg hunt today each egg  filled with their favorite treats thanks  for watching  robin gill will be here at the anchor  desk this weekend and i will see you  again next week  take care of yourselves bye  [Music]  you

